# Global instructions

## Use the GitHub CLI for GitHub resources

Always use the `gh` CLI for accessing GitHub resources instead of direct
fetch; prompt the user if you encounter an auth issue.

## Use the repo's own command runner

If a repo wraps its tooling in a task runner (`just`, `make`, a `scripts/`
entry point), work through it rather than the underlying command — `just
check`, not `pnpm turbo ...`. Check what recipes exist (`just --list`)
before reaching for the raw tool.

**Why:** the recipe is not a shortcut for the raw command — it carries what
the raw command doesn't: the right working directory, a PATH shim,
credential resolution, cache-aware orchestration. Bypassing it produces a
run that looks equivalent and isn't, and the difference tends to surface as
a confusing failure two steps later.

## Target explicitly; don't rely on ambient state

Where a tool offers a "select then act" workflow (`pulumi stack select`,
`kubectl config use-context`, `gcloud config set project`), don't use it —
pass the target on each invocation instead (`pulumi -C <dir> -s <stack>
up`). Likewise prefer absolute paths and explicit target flags over
whatever directory or context the shell was last left in.

**Why:** a "select" mutates state the *next* command inherits — in this
session, a parallel session, or the user's own shell tomorrow. An explicit
target makes each invocation self-describing and stops it quietly acting on
the wrong stack, host or directory.

## Git workflow: branch, checkpoint as you go, merge only when directed

Never commit directly to `main`/`master` unless explicitly directed to — do
the work on a branch. Group commits into logical, reviewable chunks rather
than one commit for everything, and push the branch with a draft PR at
sensible checkpoints as work progresses, rather than waiting until
everything is finished to make it visible.

When explicitly directed to complete or ship the work, squash-merge the PR
into main. Exception: a production-stage Exxo repo (a client product repo,
an infra/deploy repo) always needs human review before merge — open the PR
and stop there even if otherwise directed to complete the work, unless the
operator explicitly overrides that requirement for this instance.

An explicit direction to skip part of this flow — commit straight to main,
merge without waiting, skip the draft stage — is scoped to the specific
change it was given for, one-off. It does not carry forward to later
changes in the same session or the same repo, even ones that look similar;
default back to branch + draft PR + merge-on-direction for the next change
and wait for a fresh direction if this one is meant to skip it too.

**Why:** a draft PR at each checkpoint gives visibility into in-progress
work without forcing a decision on it, and merging only on explicit
direction keeps "merge" a deliberate act rather than a default outcome of
finishing a task. Production Exxo repos carry real user/business risk, so
the human review gate stays mandatory there regardless of how the change was
authored. Treating a one-off approval as standing permission is exactly the
inference that erodes the "explicit" part — each skip has to be re-earned,
not generalized from the last one.

## Environment/setup obstacles: surface, don't silently work around

If a task hits an environment or tooling setup issue — a package-manager
restriction (e.g. PEP 668's "externally managed environment" blocking
`pip install`), a missing dependency, a blocked install, a permissions or
config gap — don't route around it unprompted. Surface it to the user and
let them resolve it first.

Skip prompting only when resolving it is genuinely unnecessary for the task
at hand (a different path already covers the need, so fixing the blocked one
buys nothing) — but even then, still surface the issue for awareness rather
than silently absorbing it.

**Why:** a fixable local-environment quirk is not architectural evidence. Left
unexamined, it can quietly bias a technical decision — e.g. ruling out an
otherwise-reasonable approach because its dependency happened to be blocked
by a solvable config issue, rather than because the approach itself was
wrong. Surfacing it first keeps that reasoning honest, and gives the user the
chance to fix the environment rather than have a decision made around it.

## A hung-looking command is often waiting on a human approval

An apparently hung command may be blocked on an approval you can't see — a
Touch ID prompt on the operator's Mac, a device-code sign-in, a GUI
confirmation. Say so and wait rather than killing it, retrying it, or
switching to a path that dodges the prompt. For the same reason, run a
command that needs privilege escalation or interactive confirmation on its
own, not buried in a compound command — the prompt has to be legible to the
person answering it.

## Installing a missing dependency on the workstation: fine ad hoc, persist the rest

If a task needs a dependency that isn't installed on the local workstation
yet — e.g. `brew install libreoffice` to check a document's formatting — go
ahead and install it in the current session without stopping to ask, as long
as it's small, low-risk, and doesn't conflict with what's already installed.

If instead the dependency is particularly large, invasive, or sits
incongruously next to existing installables, still install it if the task
genuinely needs it — but don't leave it as undocumented host state. Persist
the install into the dotfiles repo (or whichever repo governs that class of
machine config, e.g. homelab for server-side installs) as idempotent config,
and open a PR for it. See the `dotfiles-repo` skill for exactly where a
given install gets persisted.

**Why:** most one-off dependencies are cheap, reversible, and not worth a
round-trip to ask about — this doesn't conflict with the "surface, don't
silently work around" rule above, since nothing here is actually blocked.
But an install big or unusual enough to change the workstation's shape (not
just add a small tool to it) needs to survive a reinstall or a new machine —
otherwise the workstation's real state silently diverges from what the repo
says it is, and the next reproduction from source control is wrong.

## Secrets are referenced, never written

A secret never appears as a literal value — not in a config file
"temporarily", not in a committed example or test fixture, not in a chat
message. Put a reference in its place (a 1Password item, an `esc:`/`1p:`
pointer, an env lookup) and resolve it at invocation time. Never ask the
user to paste a secret into the conversation — find the item and reference
it. Committed examples and fixtures get obviously-fake placeholders, never
a real identifier (account UUID, item UUID, key material).

The test: if this file were accidentally printed or committed, would it
expose something real? If yes, it must be a reference.

## Blanket-trusted files: the trust is in the file, not the entry

Where one permission grant covers a whole file (e.g.
`Bash(just --justfile=agent/Justfile:*)`), every entry in it runs without
approval — including entries added after the grant. So anything added to
such a file must be genuinely reusable, and read-only and
non-secret-revealing **by construction** (no `--reveal`/`--show-secrets`,
nothing that mutates resources). If unsure whether an entry is safe enough
for blanket trust, don't add it — and never add one to dodge a permission
prompt.

When a recurring task needs a capability the deny rules block, don't ask
for the deny to be lifted — add a narrowly-scoped recipe that fetches and
consumes the secret in a single in-recipe pipeline (fixed secret, fixed
consumer, nothing printed), so the session invokes it without ever seeing
the value.

## Don't code fast-moving tooling from memory

For anything that plausibly moved since training — a framework's current
major version, a provider SDK, a CLI's flags — read the docs that ship with
the installed version (the docs under `node_modules/<pkg>/`, `--help`,
`tldr <cmd>`) or the current official docs before writing code against it. Treat recall
as a hypothesis to check, and when an answer came from research, say what
it was checked against.

## Worktree closedown: preserve transcripts first

Never confirm Claude Code's built-in "clean up the worktree?" prompt, and
never remove a git worktree that hosted Claude sessions without first
relocating those sessions' transcripts — invoke the `worktree-closedown`
skill, which merges them into the primary checkout's project slug (keeping
them inspectable and resumable) and then removes the worktree properly.

**Why:** transcripts of sessions launched inside a worktree live under the
worktree's own cwd slug; removing the worktree orphans them from the resume
picker and the periodic transcript cleanup then deletes them for good.

## Orchestrator/subagent model policy

Sessions run Fable at medium effort as the orchestrator — that's the pinned
baseline (`model: fable`, `effortLevel: medium`), not something to restate
per session. Keep the main loop for coordination, judgment, decisions, and
final synthesis; delegate substantial self-contained work to subagents, and
pick each agent's model judiciously to balance effectiveness against token
cost:

- `sonnet` — routine and mechanical work: broad searches and file sweeps,
  well-specified implementation, boilerplate, mechanical migrations.
- `opus` — genuinely hard subtasks: tricky debugging, design-sensitive
  implementation, adversarial review/verification.
- omit `model` (inherit Fable) only when the subtask needs frontier
  judgment end-to-end.

Don't spawn a subagent for a trivial lookup a couple of direct tool calls
would answer.

**Why:** the orchestrator's context and judgment are the scarce resource;
fanning routine work out to cheaper models preserves both quota and the
main loop's context window. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` is deliberately
NOT set — it takes priority over the per-invocation model parameter, which
would defeat exactly this per-task selection.
